From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: wintrust: verify AppX/MSIX package signatures

WinVerifyTrust on an .msix returned TRUST_E_SUBJECT_FORM_UNKNOWN: Wine had no
subject interface package for AppX. winget refuses its community source when
the signature cannot be verified.

Add one ({0ac5df4b-ce07-4de2-b76e-23c839a09fd1}): crypt32 recognises a ZIP
named .appx/.msix, CryptSIPGetSignedDataMsg returns the PKCS#7 from
AppxSignature.p7x (after its "PKCX" tag), and the digest -- not one hash but
an "APPX" record of tagged hashes -- is checked in full, in softpub and in
CryptSIPVerifyIndirectData:

  AXPC  the archive up to the signature's local header
  AXCD  the central directory without the signature's record, then the ZIP64
        end record and locator rewritten as if it were absent, and the end
        record
  AXCT  [Content_Types].xml;  AXBM  AppxBlockMap.xml;  AXCI  the code
        integrity catalog, when present

The signature must also be the last entry, so no byte is outside it, and the
SpcSipInfo must name the AppX SIP. The layout was established against
Microsoft's own signed source2.msix and cross-checked against osslsigncode's
independent signer.

This matters beyond winget: Wine's softpub "verified" the hash of any
non-PE subject by returning success, so AppX gets its own strict check rather
than falling into that path.

